  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Nor'easter this weekend will stay far enough off\nto our east to make gusty winds Saturday and Sunday, and mainly\nlight rain for only the eastern half of the CWA. Otherwise: dry\nthru Wed.\n\nHonorable mention: Morning fog and frost will both go away\nquickly thanks to sunshine and a minor increase in wind. \n\nMain story: Our weather pattern will be dominated by a \ndeepening nor'easter off the coast. The storm is expected to \nmove up toward the southern New England Coast and Long Island \nbefore taking a bit of a westward jog. The center of the \nunusually-deep (988mb) surface low is progged to get right to \nthe NJ coastline by most models. That puts confidence high. \nHowever, the westward extent of the rain shield is still a bit \nuncertain. NAM and HRRR from 00Z runs are wetter west with light\nrain making it into UNV Sat evening. However, the dry air being\nbrought in from the N by the storm could make a sharp cutoff, \nor at least make it tough for the precip aloft to make it to the\nground the farther one moves west in the CWA. For now, we'll \nhang with the NBM PoPs and how far west it brings measurable \nprecip with very minor hourly tweaks to make them less specific \nat 30-36hrs out. The wind will be noticeably breezy and gusty \nthis weekend. But, no ill effects are foreseen from the wind.\n\nMax temps don't change much at all for the next 4 days, and then\nwarm up a little. Mins will be 4-5F milder tonight than Fri AM,\nand a little mild still Sun AM with more clouds overhead. There\ncould still be some valley fog later tonight/Sat AM. But, the\nincreasing clouds from the west should make that less common in\nthe eastern half+ of the CWA. \n\nThe have-nots (or halve-nots): The western half of the CWA will\nstay dry through at least the middle of next week when a dying \ncold front approaches from the west. Precip from that system may\ndry up before getting half way thru the CWA as it runs into a \nridge along the East Coast.",
